#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797} :activity/id #uuid "22d6d34d-749f-599b-a696-f6952b1b29b5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088944 :tutorial/content "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6cc3efa3-9929-5b62-9be5-629d74b8499c" :step/math-academy-id 593007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088945 :tutorial/content "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4056 :tutorial/title "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"} :step/id #uuid "95fd0f61-4e3f-5214-a406-a8309198f92d" :step/math-academy-id 593008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670761} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670758}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "7e7cbc14-094f-5a40-a91a-a3b62a3ad64b" :step/math-academy-id 593009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "29f2610f-f30f-5b70-bb79-098ffb891c67" :step/math-academy-id 593012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088862 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." :tutorial/id #uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14653 :tutorial/title "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b0d57580-3bcd-5a38-92ff-b1a7d2279815" :step/math-academy-id 593016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495311} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "685e843b-9372-5936-b5c3-cc142d209a6c" :step/math-academy-id 593017}] :activity/title "Introduction to First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087962}]}
